\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The axial symbol and the transverse product}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The \vocabk{normalized odd volume}{fdip-0179} is self-adjoint,
squares to #{\bbI_S}, and commutes with every represented
direction. Its sign spaces sum orthogonally to #{S}.
Take a nonzero real number #{f_c(r)} and the axial Hermitian
symbol #{-\ui\mathcal D(p)=f_c(r)\varepsilon_c}. On a nonzero
joint eigenvector with volume sign #{s} and symbol eigenvalue
#{\xi}, put #{b=\operatorname{sgn}(\xi)\operatorname{sgn}(f_c(r))}.
The increasing product over the axes other than #{c} has eigenvalue
##{(-1)^{n-c}\ui^m s b.}
Each volume sign space has a decomposition by the axial symbol.}

\proof{
\p{Reversing the ordered product #{P} uses #{n(n-1)/2} interchanges,
of parity #{m}. All direction operators are self-adjoint involutions,
so #{P^\dagger=(-1)^m P} and #{P^2=(-1)^m\bbI_S}.
Conjugation by #{\varepsilon_j} supplies #{n-1} negative signs
and fixes #{P}. The scalar #{(-\ui)^m} therefore gives
#{\omega^\dagger=\omega}, #{\omega^2=\bbI_S}, and commutation
with the algebra generated by the directions. The projections
#{(\bbI_S\pm\omega)/2} are orthogonal and add to the identity.
Since #{\varepsilon_c} commutes with them, its own two
projections split their ranges.}

\p{The periodic centered difference has Fourier multiplier
#{\ui\sin p_j} at unit spacing, with #{p_j=2\pi r_j/N_j}
as in \ref{fdip-009Q}. At spacing #{a>0} its real coefficient is
#{\sin(p_j)/a}. On a label supported on #{c}, only that coefficient
can survive. For the stated nonzero coefficient the eigenvalue
of #{\varepsilon_c} is #{\xi/f_c(r)}. It is #{1} or #{-1} since
the direction squares to the identity, hence equals #{b}.
Moving the rightmost #{\varepsilon_c} in #{P\varepsilon_c}
across the last #{n-c} factors cancels the equal pair, giving
##{\prod_{j\ne c}^{\mathrm{increasing}}\varepsilon_j
     =(-1)^{n-c}P\varepsilon_c.}
On the sign space #{P=\ui^m s\bbI}. Applying the last identity
to the joint eigenvector gives the result, including the empty
product in \ref{fdip-017A}.}
}

\p{For a list of pairwise orthogonal directions TauCeti's
\<html:span>[class]{lean-ref}{[\code{CliffordAlgebra.prod_map_ι_sq_scalar}](https://taucetiproject.github.io/TauCeti/docs/find/#doc/CliffordAlgebra.prod_map_\%25CE\%25B9_sq_scalar)}
gives the square of the product. For an odd-length spanning
list, \<html:span>[class]{lean-ref}{[\code{CliffordAlgebra.prod_map_ι_mem_center_of_odd_length}](https://taucetiproject.github.io/TauCeti/docs/find/#doc/CliffordAlgebra.prod_map_\%25CE\%25B9_mem_center_of_odd_length)}
gives centrality in the Clifford algebra. Here each direction
has quadratic value one. The adjoint and phase calculation uses
the declared Hermitian realization.}
